    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harmonyville sits in a river valley where Mill Brook joins the West River from the northeast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonyville%2C_Vermont"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Abenaki communities once maintained seasonal camps along the West River, using the fertile floodplains for sustenance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harmonyville"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Harmonyville was named in the 1830s as a counter to a nickname given to the hamlet by nearby residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harmonyville"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Scott Covered Bridge near Harmonyville is Vermont's longest wooden covered bridge at 277 feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harmonyville"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The West River Railroad enabled the efficient transport of lumber, fueling a local trade boom until the 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harmonyville"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "English colonists from Connecticut began the first permanent European-American settlement in Harmonyville around 1770.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
